<section class="article-hero"><div class="container"><div class="article-meta"><span class="article-category">AI &amp; Deal Operations</span><span>November 20, 2026</span><span>6 min read</span></div><h1>Before Claude Sends Your Next Deal Email, Decide Who Approves It</h1><p>Deal Stack Brief | PE Tech Partners · By Carl de Prado</p><p class="article-intro">A faster follow-up is useful. A buyer email sent with the wrong deal context is a problem. Before December 9, give your deal team clear rules for what Claude can do on the firm’s behalf.</p><img class="hero-image" src="attached_assets/claude-microsoft-365-actions.webp" width="1200" height="630" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" alt="A person approving email, calendar, document, and chat actions in a connected workflow"></div></section>
<main class="article-content"><div class="container"><article aria-label="Claude Microsoft 365 approvals for PE and M&amp;A teams"><p>Imagine an associate asking Claude to follow up with the buyers who have not responded to a process update. The draft looks routine. But one recipient has dropped out, another should receive a different message, and the latest timeline is still awaiting partner approval.</p>
<p>For a boutique M&amp;A advisor or a lean PE team, the value of AI is obvious: less time moving information between Outlook, Teams, and deal files. The business decision is just as clear: <strong>which actions can happen without someone checking the recipient, the content, and the timing?</strong></p>
<h2>What actually changes around December 9</h2>
<p>Anthropic says that <strong>on or after December 9, 2026</strong>, it will remove an approval limit on 11 Microsoft 365 tools for Team and Enterprise organizations that added the connector before November 9. These cover certain Outlook, calendar, and Teams actions. This is not a universal launch date for write access.</p>
<p>Saved settings remain in place. A tool explicitly set to Blocked stays blocked. Unconfigured tools affected by the change move to Needs approval; some write tools previously blocked by default can become available under that setting. Users can choose Always allow only where the admin permits it, with an additional Cowork control.</p>
<p>Some automatically approved scheduled tasks and Claude Code routines can use Always allow tools without a person present. Tasks may lose access to tools left on Needs approval. <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/12542951-set-up-the-microsoft-365-connector">Read Anthropic’s administrator guidance</a> and check your firm’s actual configuration.</p>
<div class="highlight-box"><p><strong>The decision for a deal team:</strong> identify the workflows that save time, then decide where a human must remain accountable. Do that before changing permissions.</p></div>
<h2>Start with the work that leaves the firm</h2>
<p>Our recommended starting point is human approval for external deal correspondence. That includes buyer follow-ups, seller updates, lender questions, management-team requests, and investor communications. An associate can use AI to prepare the work; the appropriate deal professional checks it before it goes out.</p>
<p>The review should cover more than grammar. Is this the right recipient for this process? Is the information current? Does the message reveal a negotiation position? Has the deal lead approved the timing? A well-written email can still be the wrong email to send.</p>
<p>Keep an equally clear review step for changes to shared diligence trackers and investment-committee materials. Separate working drafts from approved files. Name the person who owns each document and the person who can authorize a change.</p>
<h2>Use three practical categories</h2>
<p>These are suggested business rules for your firm to translate into technical controls, not built-in recipient-by-recipient restrictions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep off:</strong> actions with no agreed business use or no accountable owner. There is little benefit in enabling a capability because it is available.</li>
<li><strong>Require review:</strong> communications with buyers, sellers, lenders, and LPs; changes to live diligence records; and calendar invitations involving external participants or confidential deal names.</li>
<li><strong>Consider limited automation:</strong> narrowly defined internal reminders or routine scheduling work, after testing the recipients, content, permissions, and failure handling.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>A policy that says “internal only” is not an enforcement mechanism.</strong> Do not enable a broad send capability on the assumption that it can only reach your internal team. Have your administrator confirm how the proposed restriction will actually be enforced. If it cannot be enforced reliably, retain approval.</p>
<h2>Check what the connector can actually reach</h2>
<p>The connector acts within the user’s Microsoft 365 permissions. Anthropic says it cannot be limited to an allowlist of SharePoint sites using Sites.Selected. Its email write tools also do not currently support attachments. <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/15183774-connect-to-microsoft-365">Check the current capabilities and limitations</a> before designing a workflow around them.</p>
<p>For your firm, that means reviewing access to live deal folders before a pilot. A project name in a prompt is not a substitute for access controls. Use sanitized sample materials to test whether the workflow selects the correct transaction, recipient list, and document version. Keep confidential CIMs and negotiation materials out of an unapproved experiment.</p>
<h2>Test the Monday-morning workflows now</h2>
<p>Ask each team member what already runs on a schedule. Include pipeline summaries, follow-up reminders, calendar coordination, and recurring portfolio updates. Record the owner, the account it uses, the destination, and the action it takes.</p>
<p>Then test a normal run and a failure. What happens if approval is unavailable? Who notices if a scheduled update does not arrive? Who takes over manually? Avoid relaxing a sensitive permission simply to keep an automation running.</p>
<p>Start with one low-risk workflow and a named reviewer. Expand only after the team can explain both the benefit and the controls. That is a more useful result than turning on a long list of tools nobody owns.</p>
<h2>A second date for teams using Claude Design</h2>
<p>The standalone Claude Design site closes on <strong>December 14, 2026</strong>. Teams using it for pitch decks, one-pagers, or presentation templates should move or download work they need to keep. Chats and comments do not move with projects. Organization owners should check that the required artifacts and design-system settings are enabled. <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/17440474-move-from-standalone-claude-design-to-claude">Follow Anthropic’s migration instructions</a>.</p>
<p>Assign someone to verify the result: open the moved materials, check sharing, and confirm the approved version is available to the people preparing your next presentation.</p>
<h2>Your pre-December 9 checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Name an owner.</strong> A partner or COO owns the business rules; the administrator implements them.</li>
<li><strong>Inventory actual use.</strong> Identify connected accounts, live workflows, and unattended tasks.</li>
<li><strong>Set approval boundaries.</strong> Decide which communications and file changes require a deal lead’s review.</li>
<li><strong>Test with safe examples.</strong> Include a wrong recipient, an outdated document, and a failed scheduled run.</li>
<li><strong>Document the fallback.</strong> Make sure the team can continue working when an automation pauses.</li>
<li><strong>Move Design work if relevant.</strong> Verify saved projects and templates before December 14.</li>
</ol>
<p>Your analysts should spend more time evaluating deals and less time chasing administrative work. Clear approval rules let you pursue that benefit while keeping partners in control of the communications and decisions that represent the firm.</p></article><aside class="source-note">Product details checked against Anthropic’s documentation on November 20, 2026. Rollout timing and available controls can change. The workflow recommendations above are PE Tech Partners’ guidance.</aside></div></main>
